<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Veracruz (city)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> is the principal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, in the state of the same name. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> in 1519 as the Villa Rica de la Vera Cruz and later moved to its present site, it was the gateway of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> through which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> fleets sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, guarded by the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-de-ulua">San Juan de Ulúa</a>. It was blockaded and attacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1838, captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1847 and occupied again by American forces in 1914.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/veracruz-city.jpg" alt="Veracruz (city)" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alejandro borbolla · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_Francisco_Javier_Clavijero.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a>, also known as Heroica Veracruz, is the municipal seat of the municipality of Veracruz on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>, 90 km (56 mi) south-east of the state capital Xalapa. The area is flat, at an average altitude of ten metres above sea level, with a tropical savanna climate and a wet season that lasts from June to October. Part of the city extends into the neighbouring municipality of Boca del Río.</p><p>The name comes from the Latin Vera Crux, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a>. Hernán Cortés founded the settlement of Villa Rica on Good Friday, 22 April 1519, and dedicated it to the True Cross. It was the first Spanish settlement on the mainland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> to receive a coat of arms. The Spanish captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-grijalva">Juan de Grijalva</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernal-diaz-del-castillo">Bernal Díaz del Castillo</a>, had first arrived in 1518 at the island later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-de-ulua">San Juan de Ulúa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the colonial period the port was the most important in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, and its merchants formed a mercantile class larger and at times wealthier than that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. Turkeys, corn, beans, avocados and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> were exported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and fava beans, wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, pigs, fabric and wine came in return. From the mid sixteenth century gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> were the chief exports, carried on the treasure ships of the Spanish treasure fleet. This wealth attracted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Holland, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> and John Hawkins, and Fort San Juan de Ulúa was built against them.</p><p>After independence in 1821 the city twice earned the title of Heroic City, for its defence against a French attack during the Pastry War and against American forces in 1847. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> the siege continued until 27 March 1847, when General Juan Morales began to negotiate with General Winfield Scott, and on the 29th the city and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-de-ulua">San Juan de Ulúa</a> were occupied. About five thousand prisoners and four hundred pieces of artillery were taken. American forces attacked and occupied the port again between April and November 1914, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>, in a dispute with President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoriano-huerta">Victoriano Huerta</a>, and the city gained its fourth title.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Veracruz is the most populous city of its state, with 537,952 inhabitants at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. The Port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz">Veracruz</a> is the oldest and largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and its economy has recovered since the later twentieth century. Its culture blends indigenous, Spanish and Afro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> elements, seen in its son jarocho music and its cuisine, including red snapper Veracruz-style. The Veracruz Carnaval has been held every year since 1866, in the reign of Maximilian I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Veracruz (city)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The United States occupied <b>Veracruz</b> in 1914 and sent the Punitive Expedition after Villa in 1916, and the Cristero War of 1926–1929 pitted Catholic rebels against the state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Hernán Cortés landed on the Gulf coast in 1519, founded <b>Veracruz</b>, and with the decisive help of indigenous allies, above all the Tlaxcalans, besieged Tenochtitlan, which fell on 13 August 1521 after a smallpox epidemic had already devastated its defenders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>France blockaded <b>Veracruz</b> in the Pastry War of 1838.</blockquote><a href="/state/centralist-republic-of-mexico">Centralist Republic of Mexico</a></li><li><blockquote>Antonio López de Santa Anna rose at <b>Veracruz</b> in December 1822, and the Plan of Casa Mata in 1823 won over the army.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a></li></ul></section>
